= Software =

== Overview ==
'''Software''' is the non-physical computational material of a computer system: programs, associated data, and related executable or operational information that directs hardware to perform useful work.

The term can refer narrowly to program code or more broadly to applications, libraries, operating systems, services, configuration, data, and other artifacts needed for a computational system to behave as intended.

== Definition ==
NIST terminology defines software in several closely related ways, including computer programs and associated data that may be dynamically written or modified during execution, and the programs, procedures, rules, and associated documentation of an information-processing system.

In practical use, software is distinguished from '''hardware''', the physical components of a system, and from '''firmware''', software-like instructions or data stored in a form more tightly coupled to a device and often less dynamically modified during normal execution.

== Scope & importance ==
Software turns general-purpose or specialized hardware into systems that perform particular functions. A laptop, phone, server, vehicle controller, network router, scientific instrument, or industrial machine may use similar physical computing principles but exhibit very different behavior because of its software.

Software is larger than a single [[Program (computing)|program]]. A software system may contain many programs plus libraries, schemas, configuration, assets, build definitions, deployment logic, interfaces, tests, documentation, and external dependencies.

== Core concepts ==
Important software dimensions include:

* '''Functionality''' — what behavior or service the software provides.
* '''Architecture''' — how components and responsibilities are organized.
* '''Interfaces''' — how components, users, devices, and external systems interact.
* '''State and data''' — what information the system stores and how it changes.
* '''Dependencies''' — libraries, runtimes, services, protocols, and platforms required for operation.
* '''Quality attributes''' — reliability, security, maintainability, performance, usability, portability, safety, and other non-functional properties.
* '''Lifecycle''' — design, implementation, testing, deployment, operation, maintenance, evolution, and retirement.

== Major kinds ==
'''System software''' provides foundational services for other software. Operating systems, device drivers, runtimes, and some infrastructure tools fall into this class.

'''Application software''' performs tasks for users or organizations, such as browsers, editors, games, scientific tools, accounting systems, or communication applications.

'''Libraries and frameworks''' provide reusable functionality used by other software.

'''Embedded software''' operates within devices or machines whose primary purpose may not appear to be general-purpose computing.

'''Services''' run as network-accessible software and may be composed into distributed systems.

These categories overlap; they are descriptive rather than strict natural kinds.

== Software vs. program ==
A program is an executable expression of computational behavior. Software is the broader engineered artifact or system in which programs participate.

For a small utility, the distinction may be negligible. For a large service, operating system, browser, or database, "software" includes many programs and supporting artifacts that collectively provide the product or system behavior.

== Software construction ==
Software development commonly involves:

# understanding requirements and constraints;
# choosing representations, algorithms, data structures, and interfaces;
# writing or generating source code;
# testing and analyzing behavior;
# building and packaging artifacts;
# deploying or distributing them;
# observing operation and correcting defects;
# evolving the system as requirements and environments change.

ACM/IEEE-CS CS2023 treats software development fundamentals and software engineering as distinct but connected knowledge areas, reflecting the difference between constructing programs and engineering larger software systems over time.

== Examples ==
A text editor installed on a laptop is application software.

A Linux distribution combines an operating-system kernel, system utilities, libraries, package-management components, configuration, and applications into a larger software environment.

A cloud application may consist of browser code, APIs, worker processes, databases, infrastructure definitions, and third-party services.

A vehicle control unit may run embedded software that reads sensors, applies control logic, and communicates with other onboard systems.

== Applications / implications ==
Software is now embedded in infrastructure, finance, medicine, communication, science, transportation, manufacturing, entertainment, and governance. Because software can mediate access to data and physical systems, defects or compromised dependencies can have consequences far beyond a single computer.

Software quality therefore includes more than whether a program appears to work. Security, provenance, update mechanisms, reproducibility, interoperability, observability, and maintainability can all affect whether software remains dependable over its lifetime.

== Technical depth ==
Software engineering studies requirements, architecture, design, testing, verification, configuration management, version control, deployment, maintenance, measurement, reliability, safety, and team/process concerns.

Modern software frequently depends on layers of other software: compilers, operating systems, runtimes, package ecosystems, network services, containers, virtual machines, and cloud platforms. This layered dependency structure creates both reuse and systemic risk.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Software" is an established concept, but its boundary is contextual. Documentation, models, configuration, prompts, datasets, infrastructure definitions, and generated artifacts may be treated as part of software in some engineering contexts and as separate supporting assets in others. When scope matters, identify the concrete artifact set rather than relying on the label alone.

